<h2 id=intro>Introduction</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

Web apps show keyboard shortcuts: "Press / to search", "Ctrl+Enter to send". On a phone or a tablet
without a keyboard, those hints are clutter. '@media/pointer' and '@media/hover' say whether there is a mouse,
but a tablet with a keyboard case and no trackpad has a keyboard and no mouse, and a phone never does.

<div class=example id=example-shortcuts>
    <pre highlight=css>
    kbd.hint { display: none; }
    @media (keyboard: physical) {
      kbd.hint { display: inline; }
    }
    </pre>
</div>

Pages also struggle with on-screen keyboards. A bottom navigation bar or a "Chat with us" button that stays
fixed at the bottom of the viewport ends up on top of the keyboard, or squeezed between the keyboard and the
text the user is typing.

<div class=example id=example-footer>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @media (virtual-keyboard: visible) {
      .bottom-nav, .chat-launcher { display: none; }
      .composer { padding-block-end: 0; }
    }
    </pre>
</div>


<h2 id=keyboard-feature>The '@media/keyboard' feature</h2>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: keyboard
Value: none | physical
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

The '@media/keyboard' media feature is a [=system media feature=]. Its [=actual value=] is:

: <dfn value for=@media/keyboard>physical</dfn>
:: The user agent believes that a [=hardware keyboard=] is connected and can be used.
: <dfn value for=@media/keyboard>none</dfn>
:: Otherwise. The user may still type with an on-screen keyboard, handwriting, or dictation. This value
    evaluates to false in a [=boolean context=].

A <dfn>hardware keyboard</dfn> is a physical device that can type text and key combinations, such as a
keyboard built into a laptop, a keyboard cover attached to a tablet, or a keyboard connected over USB or
Bluetooth. These are not hardware keyboards for this feature, even when they send key events:

* Input devices used by assistive technology, such as switch devices, braille displays, and devices for
    augmentative and alternative communication.
* Game controllers and remote controls.
* Devices that send key events for other purposes, such as barcode scanners, when the user agent can tell.
* A keyboard that the operating system has turned off, such as the keyboard of a laptop folded into a tablet
    shape.

On a desktop or laptop computer, where the operating system doesn't report whether a keyboard is connected,
the user agent reports ''keyboard/physical''.

The <dfn>platform default</dfn> is the value the user agent would report on the device with nothing attached
and nothing folded: ''keyboard/physical'' on desktop and laptop computers, and ''keyboard/none'' on phones,
tablets, and other devices whose primary input is not a keyboard.

<h3 id=keyboard-rare>Devices where keyboards are rare</h3>

On a kind of device where fewer than one in a hundred users have a hardware keyboard connected, such as a
phone, the user agent should report the [=platform default=] unless the user has chosen to let sites know
when a keyboard is connected.

<div class=note>
    A user whose phone has a keyboard connected is rare enough to reveal more than 6.6 bits of information.
    See [[#privacy-fingerprinting]].
</div>

<h3 id=keyboard-changes>Changes</h3>

The '@media/keyboard' feature uses [=delayed change delivery=] [[!MEDIA-FEATURE-PRIVACY]], with a
[=maximum delay=] of 10 seconds and a [=minimum interval=] of 60 seconds.

<h3 id=keyboard-frames>Frames</h3>

The '@media/keyboard' feature uses [=frame-scoped exposure=]. Its policy-controlled feature is
"<dfn export>keyboard-state</dfn>", whose default allowlist is <code>&#39;self&#39;</code>. In a document
that isn't [=allowed to use=] it, the feature takes its [=restricted value=], the [=platform default=].

<h3 id=keyboard-reduced-exposure>Reduced exposure</h3>

The '@media/keyboard' feature is [=under user control=]. In a [=reduced-exposure context=], it is the
[=platform default=].


<h2 id=virtual-keyboard-feature>The '@media/virtual-keyboard' feature</h2>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: virtual-keyboard
Value: hidden | visible
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

The '@media/virtual-keyboard' media feature is a [=system media feature=]. In a [=document=] |d|, its
[=actual value=] is:

: <dfn value for=@media/virtual-keyboard>visible</dfn>
:: An on-screen keyboard provided by the operating system or the user agent is shown, it is typing into an
    [=editing host=] or text control in a document whose [=node navigable=]'s
    [=navigable/top-level traversable=] is the same as |d|'s, and it covers part of that top-level
    traversable's viewport, either by overlapping it or by making it smaller.
: <dfn value for=@media/virtual-keyboard>hidden</dfn>
:: Otherwise. This value evaluates to false in a [=boolean context=].

An on-screen keyboard that is typing into anything else doesn't make the feature ''virtual-keyboard/visible''.
That includes the user agent's own interface, such as its address bar, its find-in-page box, and its password
and autofill prompts; other apps, including an app sharing the screen in a split view; and the operating
system's own dialogs.

The value changes in the same rendering update in which the user agent resizes the viewport or the visual
viewport because of the keyboard, or, if neither is resized, in which it would fire the
<code>geometrychange</code> event of the VirtualKeyboard API [[VIRTUAL-KEYBOARD]].

<div class=note>
    The feature says only that the keyboard is there. Pages that need its size can use the
    <code>keyboard-inset-*</code> environment variables of the VirtualKeyboard API.
</div>

<h3 id=virtual-keyboard-frames>Frames</h3>

The '@media/virtual-keyboard' feature uses [=frame-scoped exposure=]. Its policy-controlled feature is
"<dfn export>virtual-keyboard-state</dfn>", whose default allowlist is <code>&#39;self&#39;</code>. In a
document |d| that isn't [=allowed to use=] it, the feature takes its [=restricted value=]:
''virtual-keyboard/visible'' if the feature is ''virtual-keyboard/visible'' and the keyboard is typing into an
editing host or text control in |d| itself, and ''virtual-keyboard/hidden'' otherwise.

<div class=note>
    A chat widget in a cross-origin frame learns that the keyboard is showing when the user is typing into
    the widget, which it knows anyway, because it has focus. It doesn't learn when the user types into the page
    around it.
</div>


<h2 id=privacy>Privacy considerations</h2>

<h3 id=privacy-keyboard-exposed>What '@media/keyboard' reveals</h3>

On desktop and laptop computers, the feature is ''keyboard/physical'', and reveals nothing. On phones and
tablets, it reveals:

* **An accessory.** The user owns a keyboard case, or a Bluetooth keyboard. That can suggest the model of
    the device, and on a phone it is rare.
* **How the device is being used.** A tablet with its keyboard attached is probably being used for work;
    one without is probably being used to read or watch.
* **When the keyboard is attached or detached**, which is the same moment in every open document.

The feature doesn't reveal the keyboard's layout, language, manufacturer, or connection, or how many keyboards
there are.

<h3 id=privacy-keyboard-at>Assistive technology</h3>

Many people with disabilities use input devices that behave like keyboards: switch devices, braille displays
with keys, and communication devices. Some blind people use hardware keyboards with their phones. If those
devices counted, the feature would help identify people with disabilities, and would reveal their use of
assistive technology. They don't count, and the rule for [[#keyboard-rare]] means that a hardware keyboard on a
phone isn't reported unless the user chooses.

<h3 id=privacy-keyboard-existing>What is already exposed</h3>

* Once the user types, keyboard events reveal a hardware keyboard, for example through the physical key
    codes of <code>KeyboardEvent.code</code>. The feature reveals it before the user types.
* The Keyboard Map API [[KEYBOARD-MAP]], where supported, reveals the keyboard layout, which is much more
    identifying than whether a keyboard is present.
* '@media/any-pointer' and '@media/any-hover' already reveal when a trackpad or mouse is attached, which
    many tablet keyboard cases include.

<h3 id=privacy-fingerprinting>Fingerprinting</h3>

Where most devices of a kind have the same value, the uncommon value is what identifies users. If 1% of phones
have a keyboard connected at a given moment, a phone that reports ''keyboard/physical'' reveals 6.6 bits.
On tablets, where keyboard cases are common, the same value reveals much less.

Mitigations:

* On desktop and laptop computers, the value is almost always the same.
* On kinds of devices where keyboards are rare, the platform default is reported unless the user chooses
    otherwise. See [[#keyboard-rare]].
* Cross-origin frames get the platform default unless the embedding page allows more.
* The platform default in [=reduced-exposure contexts=]. The platform default reveals only the kind of device,
    which the user-agent string already reveals.

<h3 id=privacy-keyboard-correlation>Linking visits by when the value changes</h3>

Attaching a keyboard changes the value in every open document at once. The feature uses
[=delayed change delivery=], so hidden documents learn of the change only when they become visible, and visible
documents learn of it after a random delay of up to 10 seconds, at most once a minute. Tablets, where attaching
keyboards is common, usually show one document at a time. Frame-scoped exposure keeps third-party frames, which
are the usual way trackers link visits, from seeing changes at all.

A 10-second delay is short, and [[MEDIA-FEATURE-PRIVACY]] explains that a short delay protects less. The delay is
short because the hints the feature shows should follow the keyboard promptly; the protection here comes mostly
from visibility and frame-scoped exposure.

<h3 id=privacy-virtual-exposed>What '@media/virtual-keyboard' reveals</h3>

The feature reveals that the user is typing into the page, or into a frame in it, with an on-screen keyboard,
and when the user closes the keyboard without leaving the field.

Script in the top-level document can already tell. Focus events say which field the user is in, and on most
phones the visual viewport shrinks when the keyboard appears, which <code>visualViewport</code> reports. The
feature changes in the same rendering update as that resize.

The feature doesn't reveal the keyboard's size, which would reveal the keyboard app and its language, or whether
the keyboard is the system's or a third-party app's. It doesn't reveal typing into the user agent's interface,
such as the address bar, which would reveal that the user was about to leave the page, or typing into other
apps.

<h3 id=privacy-virtual-third-party>Embedded content</h3>

A cross-origin frame learns only about the keyboard typing into its own document, which it knows from focus. It
doesn't learn when the user types into the page that embeds it.

<h3 id=privacy-without-script>Reading the features without script</h3>

A style sheet can report the value of either feature to a server by loading a resource. For '@media/keyboard',
this reveals the same thing as script would, at page load or after a delay; documents where script is disabled
for privacy reasons are [=reduced-exposure contexts=], where it is the platform default. For
'@media/virtual-keyboard', a style sheet can already tell that the user is typing, with ':focus'.

<h3 id=privacy-residual>Remaining risks</h3>

* On tablets, where both values are common, '@media/keyboard' reveals whether a keyboard is attached, and
    documents visible when it changes can be linked with more effort than frames.
* A user who chooses to reveal a keyboard on their phone reveals a rare value.


<h2 id=security>Security considerations</h2>

* Neither feature can type, read keystrokes, or open or close a keyboard.
* **Instructions that need a keyboard.** Some attacks tell users to press key combinations that run commands,
    such as opening a system dialog and pasting a command into it. A page could show those instructions only to
    users with a hardware keyboard, and keep them from phones and from scanners that don't emulate one. The
    user-agent string already reveals the operating system, which is what those attacks target, and desktop
    computers always report ''keyboard/physical''. Security scanners should check pages with both values.
* **Fake keyboards.** A page could draw its own keyboard to capture what the user types, and use the feature to
    do so only when the real keyboard is hidden. Pages can already draw anything. The operating system draws the
    real keyboard above the page, and the page can't hide it.
* **Moving targets.** A page that moves its controls when the keyboard appears could move a button under the
    user's finger. Pages can already do this when the viewport resizes. Security-sensitive controls, such as
    payment confirmation, shouldn't move when the keyboard appears.


<h2 id=alternate-proposals>Alternate proposals</h2>

<h3 id=alt-layout>Keyboard layout or language</h3>

A feature for the layout, such as <code>(keyboard-layout: azerty)</code>, would let pages show the right keys
in hints. It was not chosen because the layout identifies users far more than the presence of a keyboard, and
the Keyboard Map API already provides it to script where it is supported.

<h3 id=alt-env>Only the environment variables</h3>

The <code>keyboard-inset-*</code> environment variables can already describe the keyboard's area, but only when
the page opts into having the keyboard overlay its content, and they don't work in media queries or
<code>&lt;source media></code>. '@media/virtual-keyboard' works with the page's normal layout.

<h3 id=alt-any-keyboard>''any-keyboard'' and ''keyboard'' like ''any-pointer'' and ''pointer''</h3>

A pair of features could describe the primary keyboard and any keyboard. Devices rarely have more than one kind,
and a second feature would reveal more for little benefit.


<h2 id=open-issues>Open issues</h2>

<p class=issue>Is one in a hundred the right threshold for [[#keyboard-rare]], and how should user agents
estimate it without collecting data about their users' keyboards?

<p class=issue>Should '@media/keyboard' be ''keyboard/none'' on a laptop whose built-in keyboard is covered or
disconnected, when the operating system doesn't report it?


<h2 id=questionnaire class=no-num>Appendix: Security and privacy questionnaire</h2>

*This section is non-normative.* These are the answers to the questions in
[[SECURITY-PRIVACY-QUESTIONNAIRE]].

: What information does this feature expose, and for what purposes?
:: Whether a hardware keyboard is available, so pages can show shortcuts, and whether an on-screen keyboard is
    covering the page, so pages can move content out of its way.
: Do features in your specification expose the minimum amount of information necessary?
:: Yes. Two values each, and no layout, language, size, or device.
: Do the features expose personal information, PII, or information derived from either?
:: No. Assistive technology devices are excluded so that disability isn't revealed.
: How do the features deal with sensitive information?
:: See [[#privacy-keyboard-at]].
: Does data exposed carry related but distinct information that may not be obvious to users?
:: On phones and tablets, that the user owns a keyboard, and how they are using the device.
: Do the features introduce state that persists across browsing sessions?
:: No.
: Do the features expose information about the underlying platform to origins?
:: Whether a keyboard is connected, and whether the on-screen keyboard is shown for the page.
: Does this specification allow an origin to send data to the underlying platform?
:: No.
: Do features enable access to device sensors?
:: No.
: Do features enable new script execution or loading mechanisms?
:: No.
: Do features allow an origin to access other devices?
:: No. It learns whether a keyboard is connected, and nothing else about it.
: Do features allow an origin some measure of control over a user agent's native UI?
:: No.
: What temporary identifiers do the features create or expose to the web?
:: The moment a keyboard is attached or detached. See [[#privacy-keyboard-correlation]].
: How does this specification distinguish between behavior in first-party and third-party contexts?
:: Cross-origin frames get the platform default for '@media/keyboard', and only learn about their own input
    for '@media/virtual-keyboard', unless the embedding page allows more.
: How do the features work in the context of a browser's Private Browsing or Incognito mode?
:: In modes that protect against fingerprinting, '@media/keyboard' is the platform default.
    '@media/virtual-keyboard' is unchanged, because script sees the viewport resize.
: Does this specification have both "Security Considerations" and "Privacy Considerations" sections?
:: Yes.
: Do features enable origins to downgrade default security protections?
:: No.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature is kept alive in BFCache?
:: When restored, it gets the current value of '@media/keyboard' as it becomes visible. The on-screen keyboard
    isn't typing into a cached document, so '@media/virtual-keyboard' is ''virtual-keyboard/hidden''.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature gets disconnected?
:: Nothing is kept.
: Does your spec define when and how new kinds of errors should be raised?
:: No.
: Does your feature allow sites to learn about the user's use of assistive technology?
:: No. Devices used by assistive technology aren't hardware keyboards for this feature.
: What should this questionnaire have asked?
:: Whether a value that is common on one kind of device is rare, and therefore identifying, on another.
